4,8,5,4,5,8,4,2,2,4,8,5,4,5,8,4,2,2,4,8,5],[9,5,3,3,5,9,6,5,6,9,5,3,3,5,9,6,5,6,9,5,3,3,5,9,6],[1,6,4,4,6,1,7,6,7,1,6,4,4,6,1,7,6,7,1,6,4,4,6,1,7],[2,7,5,5,7,2,8,7,8,2,7,5,5,7,2,8,7,8,2,7,5,5,7,2,8],[3,8,6,6,8,3,9,8,9,3,8,6,6,8,3,9,8,9,3,8,6,6,8,3,9],[4,9,7,7,9,4,1,9,1,4,9,7,7,9,4,1,9,1,4,9,7,7,9,4,1],[5,1,8,8,1,5,2,1,2,5,1,8,8,1,5,2,1,2,5,1,8,8,1,5,2],[6,2,9,9,2,6,3,2,3,6,2,9,9,2,6,3,2,3,6,2,9,9,2,6,3],[7,3,1,1,3,7,4,3,4,7,3,1,1,3,7,4,3,4,7,3,1,1,3,7,4],[8,4,2,2,4,8,5,4,5,8,4,2,2,4,8,5,4,5,8,4,2,2,4,8,5],[9,5,3,3,5,9,6,5,6,9,5,3,3,5,9,6,5,6,9,5,3,3,5,9,6],[1,6,4,4,6,1,7,6,7,1,6,4,4,6,1,7,6,7,1,6,4,4,6,1,7]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"**What the grids are made of.** Every input is a repeating wallpaper with three properties:\n- Each row is the row above with every colour increased by one, wrapping around the colour cycle.\n- Each row repeats horizontally. The period is 7 in pair 1, 6 in pair 2, 4 in pair 3 and 9 in the test.\n- Solid rectangles of colour 1 are pasted over the wallpaper. In pair 1 these are at rows 9–11, columns 4–9, and at rows 17–20, columns 2–6.\n\nThe output is the same wallpaper with those rectangles removed. The fact sheet confirms this: every change is \"1 → something\", and nothing else moves.\n\n**What makes it tricky.** Colour 1 is also a genuine wallpaper colour; for example, row 3 of pair 1 reads `3 1 1 3 …`. So I cannot treat every 1 as damage. Instead, I treat 1 as \"unknown\" and recover the pattern from the cells that are certainly genuine, the non-1 cells.\n\n**Finding the periods.** For a candidate horizontal period p, I compare each cell with the cell p columns to its right. I skip the pair if either cell is 1. The period is the smallest p with zero disagreements and at least some pairs compared. The vertical period is found the same way. Because each row is the previous row shifted by +1, the vertical period equals the number of colours in the cycle (7, 6, 8 and 9).\n\n**Repairing.** Every cell belongs to a residue class (y mod py, x mod px). For each class, I take the most common non-1 value among its cells. If a class contains only 1s, its true colour is 1.\n\nEach class has many members: with 22 rows and 25 columns there are at least 2×2, and usually 3×3 or more. The rectangles are small, so no class is entirely covered by them.\n\n**Checking against the pairs.** Any cell of a rectangle whose true colour is 1 stays 1. Every other covered cell gets the colour its unmasked copies show, which is exactly what each train output shows.\n\n**On the test.** The horizontal period is 9 and there are 9 colours. The repair fills four 1-blocks:\n- rows 5–8, columns 8–12;\n- rows 9–14, columns 3–7;\n- rows 10–12, columns 11–12;\n- an extra stray cell at (7,7).\n\n**Teaching stages.** The program emits one stage per repaired connected region, then the final grid.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    unk = 1\n\n    def find_period(axis):\n        n = g.shape[axis]\n        for p in range(1, n):\n            if axis == 1:\n                a, b = g[:, :W - p], g[:, p:]\n            else:\n                a, b = g[:H - p, :], g[p:, :]\n            m = (a != unk) & (b != unk)\n            if m.sum() > 0 and np.all(a[m] == b[m]):\n                return p\n        return n\n\n    py, px = find_period(0), find_period(1)\n    from collections import Counter\n    tile = np.full((py, px), unk, dtype=int)\n    for ry in range(py):\n        for rx in range(px):\n            vals = g[ry::py, rx::px].ravel()\n            vals = vals[vals != unk]\n            if len(vals):\n                tile[ry, rx] = Counter(vals.tolist()).most_common(1)[0][0]\n    full = np.array([[tile[y % py, x % px] for x in range(W)] for y in range(H)])\n\n    out = g.copy()\n    diff = out != full\n    for comp in components(diff, diagonal=False):\n        for (y, x) in comp:\n            out[y, x] = full[y, x]\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = bbox(comp)\n        emit(out, f\"Restore the masked region rows {y0}-{y1}, cols {x0}-{x1} from the repeating pattern (period {py}x{px}).\")\n    emit(out, \"All masks removed; the periodic wallpaper is complete.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The period is the smallest p with zero disagreements and at least some pairs compared."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"For each class, I take the most common non-1 value among its cells."}],"movement":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Because each row is the previous row shifted by +1, the vertical period equals the number of colours in the cycle (7, 6, 8 and 9)."}],"repeat":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The period is 7 in pair 1, 6 in pair 2, 4 in pair 3 and 9 in the test."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Finding the periods.** For a candidate horizontal period p, I compare each cell with the cell p columns to its right."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The period is the smallest p with zero disagreements and at least some pairs compared."}],"repair":[{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Restore the masked region rows 5-7, cols 20-22 from the repeating pattern (period 7x7)."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Restore the masked region rows 9-11, cols 4-8 from the repeating pattern (period 7x7)."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Restore the masked region rows 13-14, cols 3-5 from the repeating pattern (period 7x7)."}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Because each row is the previous row shifted by +1, the vertical period equals the number of colours in the cycle (7, 6, 8 and 9)."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"a3f60c400ea329c4df58f232f6acc825ce4cc6babeecc7444fa123e9ee310e5d","task_sha256":"325c674800043ca226964bb1a440f18d168f344193b82a7cbacb536d0765742c","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
